¶
'''avener''' (av′ẹ̄-nėr), ''n.'' [ME. ''avener'', ''avenere'', < OF. ''avenier'', < L. ''avenarius'': see ''avenary''.] In ''feudal law'', a chief officer of the stable, whose duty it was to provide oats. Also spelled ''avenor''.

'''avenge''' (ạ-venj′), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''avenged'', ppr. ''avenging''. [< ME. ''avengen'', < OF. ''avengier'', < ''a-'' (< L. ''ad'', to) + ''vengier'', revenge, take vengeance, < L. ''vindicare'', lay claim to, punish: see ''vindicate'', and cf. ''revenge'' and ''vengeance''.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To vindicate by inflicting pain or evil on the wrong-doer; execute justice or vengeance on behalf of: with a person as object.
{{smaller block|''Avenge'' me of mine adversary. {{right|Luke xviii. 3.}}
<poem>''Avenge'', O Lord, thy slaughter’d saints, whose bones
Lie scatter’d on the Alpine mountains cold.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', Sonnets, xiii.}}}}
'''2.''' To take satisfaction for, by pain or punishment inflicted on the injuring party; deal punishment on account of: with a thing as object.
{{smaller block|He will ''avenge'' the blood of his servants.
{{right|Deut. xxxii. 43.}}
<poem>Never, till Cæsar’s three-and-thirty wounds
Be well ''aveng’d''.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', J. C., v. 1.}}
I thought ten thousand swords must have leaped from their scabbards to ''avenge'' even a look that threatened her with insult. But the age of chivalry is gone.
{{right|''Burke'', Rev. in France.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
¶
'''3†.''' To take revenge on; treat or deal with revengefully.

{{smaller block|If Cain shall be ''avenged'' sevenfold, truly Lamech seventy and seven fold.
{{right|Gen. iv. 24.}}}}

{{smaller block|='''Syn.''' ''Avenge'', ''Revenge''. Until lately these words were used with little or no difference of meaning (see quotations under each). ''Avenge'' is now restricted to the taking of just punishment or the vindication of justice, and ''revenge'' to the infliction of pain or evil to gratify resentful feelings, or the desire of retaliation for some real or fancied wrong. Poetic use sometimes returns to the earlier freedom in the meaning of ''avenge''. See ''revenge'', ''n.''}}

{{smaller block|<poem>I will ''avenge'' this insult, noble Queen.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Geraint.}}
If you poison us, do we not die? and if you wrong us, shall we not ''revenge?''
{{right|''Shak.'', M. of V., iii. 1.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To execute vengeance; inflict retaliatory pain or injury on a wrong-doer.

{{smaller block|Thou shalt not ''avenge'' nor bear any grudge against the children of thy people.
{{right|Lev. xix. 18.}}
<poem>The ''avenging'' horror of a conscious mind,
Whose deadly fear anticipates the blow,
And sees no end of punishment and woe.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', tr. of Lucretius, iii. 231.}}}}

'''avenge†''' (a̤-venj′), ''n.'' [< ''avenge'', ''v.''] '''1.''' Revenge; retaliation.

{{smaller block|<poem>That ''avenge'' by you decreed.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., IV. vi. 8.}}}}

'''2.''' Punishment; vengeance taken.

{{smaller block|Why doth mine hand from thine ''avenge'' abstaine?
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., IV. i. 52.}}}}

